# ===========================================================================
#  PhytoScope — who reviews what
# ===========================================================================
#  The project is carried by one person: everything comes back to them. This
#  file is therefore not a division of labour, it is a net — it guarantees
#  that no change merges without somebody having seen it, and it makes the
#  division possible the day there is somebody else.
# ===========================================================================

*                       @thierryg

# --- What is not changed lightly -------------------------------------------
#  The requirements are authoritative: a constraint that changes changes the
#  project.
/constraints.md         @thierryg

#  The version and the attribution are the ONLY sources of truth, read by the
#  software, the package factory and the headers.
/src/phytoscope/phytoscope/VERSION   @thierryg
/src/phytoscope/phytoscope/AUTHORS   @thierryg

#  The signing chain and the certificate. The path said `/certificat/` until
#  2026-09-23 — a directory that has never existed, so the rule protected
#  nothing. CODEOWNERS is silent about a path that matches no file.
/packaging/signature.py @thierryg
/certificate/           @thierryg

#  The licences, and what stamps them.
/LICENSE                @thierryg
/LICENSES/              @thierryg
/tools/headers.py       @thierryg

#  The module contract: a third-party module depends on it (C-49).
/src/phytoscope/phytoscope/api/      @thierryg

#  Continuous integration: a workflow is code with rights.
/.github/               @thierryg
/.gitignore             @thierryg
